  "report_text": "# Initial rates: dataset.csv\n\n## Verdict\n\n- [treated] Michaelis-Menten, the only law fitted.\n- [treated] Michaelis-Menten: Every substrate concentration is well below Km: the data determine only Vmax/Km = 5163 (95% interval 1226 to 5.39e+11 counts/min/min per ppm), and neither constant alone. Km: not determined by these data in either direction; Vmax > 55.7 counts/min/min: the data do not determine an upper bound. Estimates of either are not reported, because any pair with that ratio fits these data equally well.\n\n## The data\n\n4 rate(s) at 2 distinct condition(s) (2 replicate row(s)), in 1 groups by 'state': treated (4).\nUnits: substrate in ppm (not convertible to molar: fitted as given, and not comparable with a cited constant); rate in counts/min/min (an arbitrary unit: fitted as given).\nUncertainty: estimated from the fit's own residuals (ordinary least squares), which assumes the model is right.\n\n## Michaelis-Menten (treated)\n\n`v = Vmax [S] / (Km + [S])`\n\n| constant | estimate | standard error | 95% profile interval | unit |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Vmax | not determined | - | > 55.7 (no upper bound) | counts/min/min |\n| Km | not determined | - | not bounded either way | ppm |\n| Vmax/Km | 5163 | 2440 | 1226 to 5.39e+11 | counts/min/min per ppm |\n\n- Residual standard error 15.34 counts/min/min on 2 degrees of freedom. No goodness-of-fit chi-square is given: sigma was estimated from these residuals, so the chi-square is 2 by construction and cannot test the law it was computed from.\n- Correlations of the estimates: Vmax-Km +0.959.\n- Starts: 6 of 6 reached this minimum; no start found a different one.\n- Condition number of the weighted Jacobian (log constants): 9.87.\n- Lack of fit: not tested (it needs replicates, and more distinct conditions than constants).\n- Substrate range: Km is not determined in either direction, so no range can be recommended from these data.\n\n## The other laws (treated)\n\n\n\nsubstrate inhibition (Haldane): not fitted -- the substrate inhibition (Haldane) law has 3 constant(s) (Vmax, Km, Ksi) and these rows hold 2 distinct condition(s). Replicates shrink the error on one rate; they do not add a second shape for the law to match, so at least 3 different substrate (and inhibitor) concentrations are needed.\n\nHill: not fitted -- the Hill law has 3 constant(s) (Vmax, K0.5, n) and these rows hold 2 distinct condition(s). Replicates shrink the error on one rate; they do not add a second shape for the law to match, so at least 3 different substrate (and inhibitor) concentrations are needed.\n\n## What these numbers are\n\n- FITTED to this file's rates, under the law named, not measured constants: their provenance is this dataset and this law, and they are conditional on both.\n- Intervals are 95% profile-likelihood intervals (Bates & Watts 1988); the standard errors are asymptotic and are what a +-2 SE interval would be built from, which for Km is often too symmetric.\n- A fit this good is not evidence that the law is right: no other law was tested against it here, and a law not fitted was not considered.\n- References: Cornish-Bowden A. Fundamentals of Enzyme Kinetics, 4th ed. Wiley-Blackwell, 2012 (rate laws). Segel IH. Enzyme Kinetics. Wiley, 1975 (inhibition laws). Bates DM, Watts DG. Nonlinear Regression Analysis and Its Applications. Wiley, 1988 (profile intervals). Self SG, Liang KY. J. Am. Stat. Assoc. 82:605 (1987) (tests on the boundary). Draper NR, Smith H. Applied Regression Analysis. Wiley (lack of fit and pure error). Hurvich CM, Tsai CL. Biometrika 76:297 (1989) (AICc). Brooks HB et al. Basics of Enzymatic Assays for HTS. Assay Guidance Manual, NCBI Bookshelf NBK92007 (the 0.2-5 Km design range).\n",
  "methods": "Initial rates (4 measurements at 2 distinct conditions, in 1 groups; substrate in ppm, rate in counts/min/min) were fitted to the Michaelis-Menten rate law (Cornish-Bowden 2012; Segel 1975) by nonlinear least squares (scipy.optimize.least_squares, trust-region reflective, on the logarithms of the constants, which keeps them positive; derivatives by the complex step; multiple starting points spread across the range of the data). Rates were weighted equally (ordinary least squares) and the standard deviation was estimated from the residuals, which assumes the rate law is correct. 95% confidence intervals are profile-likelihood intervals (Bates & Watts 1988), bounded by the profile-t statistic against Student's t on the residual degrees of freedom; asymptotic standard errors are also reported. The table (4 measurements used) was read by Caterva Studio from the file treated-low.csv (one measurement per row). Software: Caterva 0.5.0 (caterva rates), Python 3.12.13, NumPy 2.2.6, SciPy 1.15.3.\n",
  "cite": "Caterva 0.5.0, `caterva rates`, Apache-2.0, https://github.com/math12345678/caterva. Cite the version you used; the run's bundle records it.",
